1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
Plugins are how you extend Cloud Code without rebuilding anything.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:09,000
One package can drop in new commands, skills, and integrations at once,

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:11,000
but there are a handful of moving parts to understand.

4
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:14,000
This lesson walks through what a plugin contains,

5
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:19,000
how marketplaces feed installation, and the commands you use day-to-day.

6
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:25,000
What actually is a plugin? At its core, it's a self-contained directory

7
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:29,000
that extends Cloud Code with new capabilities,

8
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:32,000
packaged so you install the whole thing as one unit.

9
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:38,000
Inside that directory sits a manifest, the plugin.json file,

10
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:42,000
and that's what identifies the plugin and declares what it provides.

11
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:44,000
You don't handwire anything.

12
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:47,000
The manifest tells Cloud Code what's in the package.

13
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:49,000
And where do plugins come from?

14
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:54,000
A marketplace, which is just a catalog that lists available plugins

15
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:58,000
so you can browse and install from a source you trust.

16
00:00:58,000 --> 00:00:59,000
Hold those three ideas together,

17
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:03,000
the directory, the manifest, and the marketplace,

18
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:06,000
and the rest of plugin management falls into place pretty naturally.

19
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:11,000
Let's talk about what a plugin can actually bring to the table,

20
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:13,000
because it's more than just slash commands.

21
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:17,000
A single plugin can bundle several kinds of capability at once.

22
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:21,000
It can add slash commands and skills that give you new workflows.

23
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:25,000
It can bring sub-agents and hooks that run automatically as you work.

24
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:31,000
And it can package MCP servers, LSP servers for language intelligence,

25
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:33,000
and background monitors that watch for things.

26
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:36,000
The point is that these all travel together.

27
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:39,000
Because everything lives in one package,

28
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:42,000
it installs as a set and updates as a set.

29
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:45,000
You're not chasing down five separate pieces.

30
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:46,000
You add the plugin,

31
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:50,000
and every capability it carries comes online at the same time.

32
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:55,000
Here's a distinction that trips people up constantly.

33
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:56,000
So let's nail it down.

34
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:00,000
A marketplace and a plugin aren't the same thing,

35
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:02,000
and the commands treat them separately.

36
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:06,000
A marketplace is the catalog you register first.

37
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:10,000
When you add one, Cloud Code can now see the plugins it offers.

38
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:11,000
But here's the key part.

39
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:15,000
Adding the marketplace installs nothing on its own.

40
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:17,000
It's just registering where plugins live.

41
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:20,000
A plugin is the individual capability,

42
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:23,000
and installing it is a distinct second step.

43
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:27,000
So the flow is always two moves, register the catalog,

44
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:29,000
then install from it.

45
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:33,000
If you add a marketplace and wonder why nothing new showed up,

46
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:34,000
that's expected.

47
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:37,000
You still have to install the specific plugin you want.

48
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:41,000
Let's make that concrete with the actual commands.

49
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:44,000
Getting a plugin in place is a two-step flow,

50
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:47,000
and you run both steps from the plugin commands.

51
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:50,000
First, you add the marketplace.

52
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:54,000
Pointing it at a repo, a URL, or even a local path,

53
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:55,000
and that registers the catalog.

54
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:58,000
Then you install the plugin by name,

55
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:01,000
scoped to the marketplace it came from.

56
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:04,000
So Cloud Code knows exactly which one you mean.

57
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:06,000
One convenience worth knowing,

58
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:10,000
the official marketplace is already available out of the box.

59
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:13,000
So you only add the community or custom ones yourself.

60
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:15,000
Once the install finishes,

61
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:19,000
the plugins, commands, and skills are live and ready for you to use.

62
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:22,000
Once you've got plugins installed,

63
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:26,000
a small set of commands covers day-to-day management.

64
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:28,000
The list command is your inventory.

65
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:30,000
It shows every plugin you have installed

66
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:34,000
and whether each one is currently enabled or disabled.

67
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:38,000
And you can filter to just the enabled or just the disabled ones.

68
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:43,000
Enable and disable let you turn a plugin on or off in place.

69
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:47,000
The nice thing about disabling is that it keeps the plugin installed.

70
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:51,000
So flipping it back on later is instant with no re-download.

71
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:54,000
And when you're genuinely done with a capability,

72
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:57,000
uninstall removes the plugin and its files completely.

73
00:03:57,000 --> 00:03:59,000
So think of it as a spectrum.

74
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:02,000
Disable to pause and uninstall to remove for good.

75
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:06,000
There's also a visual way to manage all of this.

76
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:10,000
If you run the plugin command on its own with no arguments,

77
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:12,000
it opens an interactive manager.

78
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:14,000
And it's organized into four tabs.

79
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:16,000
The first is Discover,

80
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:20,000
where you browse plugins you could add from your registered marketplaces.

81
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:22,000
The second is Installed,

82
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:25,000
which shows what you already have and the state each one is in.

83
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:28,000
The third is Marketplaces,

84
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:30,000
listing the catalogs you've registered so far.

85
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:32,000
And the fourth is Errors,

86
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:36,000
which surfaces any plugins that failed to load.

87
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:38,000
So you have one place to go when something isn't working.

88
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:40,000
For a lot of people,

89
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:44,000
this interactive view is easier than remembering every individual command.

90
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:48,000
The word configuring is in the title of this lesson for a reason.

91
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:50,000
So let's touch on it.

92
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:52,000
When you install a plugin,

93
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:53,000
you choose a scope.

94
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:55,000
And that decides who the plugin is for,

95
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:58,000
whether it's just you or the whole project.

96
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:02,000
We'll go deep on scopes in the next lesson. Beyond that,

97
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:06,000
some plugins accept configuration values that tune how they behave.

98
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:08,000
And those get stored.

99
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:11,000
So the settings stick from one session to the next.

100
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:16,000
Now, one command ties all of this together. Reload plugins.

101
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:20,000
Whenever you enable, disable, or install something mid-session,

102
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:22,000
Reload plugins applies the change immediately.

103
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:29,000
So you don't have to restart Cloud Code just to pick up your new setup. Before we wrap,

104
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:31,000
let's clear up a source of confusion.

105
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:33,000
In a Cloud Code project,

106
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:35,000
three different things shape behavior,

107
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:36,000
and it's easy to lump them together.

108
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:39,000
Plugins add new capabilities.

109
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:41,000
The actual commands, skills,

110
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:43,000
and tools you can invoke.

111
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:45,000
Cloud.md is different.

112
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:49,000
It gives natural language instructions that Cloud reads as context,

113
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:51,000
guidance rather than machinery.

114
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:55,000
And settings.json is different again.

115
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:59,000
It sets permissions and other machine-readable options,

116
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:00,000
like what Cloud is allowed to do.

117
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:04,000
So the same project can have all three at once.

118
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:06,000
Plugins for capability.

119
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:08,000
Cloud.md for instruction.

120
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:12,000
And settings.json for configuration.

121
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:16,000
Keeping those roles straight in your head saves a lot of head-scratching

122
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:20,000
when you're deciding where a given change actually belongs.

123
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:25,000
A plugin bundles new capabilities into one installable unit.

124
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:28,000
It matters because registering a marketplace

125
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:30,000
and installing a plugin are separate steps,

126
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:34,000
and adding a catalog installs nothing by itself.

127
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:40,000
Just remember, plugins, Cloud.md, and settings.json each do a different job.

128
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:43,000
That covers installing and configuring plugins.

129
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:44,000
I'll see you in the next one.

